{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3158","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3158","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3158\",\"parent_revision\":\"c013ab4e58814d7621656341cc0e8eeba4447521\",\"root_revision\":\"fce2be13211cad0dac82476ae0aa947d6755da2190e02fba712cd189389236b4\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-22\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`backlog_core/artifact_provider.py` presents two mutually exclusive artifact-content storage designs at once, and the one that is dead is the one that reads as authoritative.\\n\\nObserved while correcting agent guidance about artifact size limits.\\n\\n`_build_artifact_content_comment` embeds artifact content in a GitHub issue comment, enforces `_GITHUB_COMMENT_MAX_CHARS = 65_536`, and on overflow returns `content[:max_content]` plus an HTML notice appended into the stored body. It has no production caller. The only references anywhere are its own definition, `tests/test_artifact_content_storage.py`, and generated graph output. Two of those tests assert the truncation behaviour, which makes the function look maintained and current.\\n\\nThe live GitHub path is `GitHubGistArtifactProvider.store_artifact_content`, which writes the content to a file in a linked Gist via `gist.edit(files={filename: InputFileContent(content)})`. It performs no size check and no truncation.\\n\\nSo a reader of this module \u2014 human or agent \u2014 encounters a documented 64 KiB cap with silent truncation, backed by passing tests, that governs nothing; and the path that actually stores content has no stated size behaviour at all.\\n\\nConsequences already produced:\\n\\n- Agent guidance was written this session telling an agent that the content provider caps a single payload and drops the remainder without reporting it, and to read the artifact back to detect it. That was derived from the dead function and is not established for the live path.\\n- If the comment-based storage is ever re-wired, the truncation is silent by construction: the notice goes into the stored body, and nothing is added to the operation's returned `warnings`, so the calling agent cannot observe that its document was cut. That is the failure shape `.claude/rules/silent-failure-prevention.md` exists to prevent.\\n\\nNot established, and needing an answer before any fix:\\n\\n- Which provider is actually selected in a default GitHub-backed deployment. `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` describes a GitHub writable-records design using content-addressed storage on blob SHA, and states it replaced an earlier Gist-backed store. If that is the live path, then `GitHubGistArtifactProvider` may itself be superseded, and this module would hold three storage designs rather than two.\\n- Whether the Gist path or the CAS path has any effective size limit, and what either does on overflow.\\n- Whether `_build_artifact_content_comment` and its tests should be deleted, or re-wired as a fallback.\\n\\nThe immediate hazard is not the cap value. It is that the module's most explicit, most tested statement about content size describes code nothing calls, so anyone reasoning about artifact size limits from this source reaches a conclusion about the wrong path.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 2026-08-23, while correcting agent guidance about artifact size limits\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-22\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Correction Not A Documentation Gap The Architecture Spec Already Answers This\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` documents the live design in full, under \\\"Provider-owned artifacts and plans\\\" and \\\"GitHub writable records\\\". Every question raised above is answered there:\\n\\n- The `dh-content` branch, the `.dh/content/v1/` layout, `_GitHubContentsStore._CONTENT_BRANCH`, why a non-default branch is required, and the bootstrap race handling are all specified.\\n- `create_artifact_provider()` is identified as migration debt: \\\"The existing independent `create_artifact_provider()` calls in `operations.py` and `server.py`, including the server's `LocalFilesystemArtifactProvider` fallback, are migration debt. They must be replaced by artifact capabilities obtained from the configured backend.\\\"\\n- The Gist path is a read-only migration representation: \\\"Native records take precedence over read-only Gist/index migration records\\\", and \\\"Neither representation uses cooperative locks, Gist read-modify-write, unconditional Issue-body updates, or a second caller-selected provider.\\\"\\n- The size limit is stated and is fail-closed: \\\"The provider rejects an encoded envelope above 1 MiB before network I/O; larger artifacts require a future provider representation rather than silently crossing GitHub's normal Contents API mode.\\\"\\n\\nSo the earlier framing in this issue \u2014 that three designs coexist with the authoritative one named only in a module docstring \u2014 is wrong. The authoritative design is specified in the package's architecture document, which `AGENTS.md` routes to by task type.\\n\\n### What remains a real defect\\n\\nThe migration the specification mandates is incomplete, and its residue is still reachable and still reads as current:\\n\\n- `create_artifact_provider()` remains, and returns `GitHubGistArtifactProvider` for the `github` backend. The spec says these calls must be replaced by capabilities obtained from the configured backend.\\n- `_build_artifact_content_comment` and its 64 KiB truncating cap remain, with no caller and with tests asserting the truncation.\\n\\nBoth are described as debt in the specification and behave as live code to anyone reading the module. Neither carries anything in the code marking it superseded.\\n\\n### Corrected scope\\n\\nThis issue is not \\\"which storage design is live\\\" \u2014 that is documented. It is: complete the migration the architecture spec mandates, and remove or mark the superseded paths so the module stops presenting retired designs as current.\\n\\nEvidence that the residue actively misleads: while filing this issue, the live path was identified incorrectly twice from the code alone \u2014 first as the comment-based cap, then as the Gist provider \u2014 before the architecture document was read. Both wrong answers were reached by reading the most prominent and best-tested code in the module.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Correction It Is Three Designs And The Live One Is In Neither Of The Files Above\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nObserved directly rather than inferred. The repository has a `dh-content` branch accumulating one commit per artifact write, each titled \\\"Store artifact_content\\\", under `.dh/content/v1/_artifact_content/{item}/...`. It is being written during normal operation.\\n\\n`backlog_core/backends/github_contents.py` sets `_CONTENT_BRANCH = \\\"dh-content\\\"`. `backlog_core/backends/github_backend.py` names `_GitHubContentsStore` \\\"the authoritative\\\" store in its own module docstring and constructs it in `GitHubBackend.__init__`.\\n\\nSo the inventory is:\\n\\n1. `artifact_provider._build_artifact_content_comment` \u2014 issue-comment storage, 64 KiB cap, silent truncation, extensively tested, no caller.\\n2. `artifact_provider.GitHubGistArtifactProvider.store_artifact_content` \u2014 Gist storage, no size handling. Returned by `create_artifact_provider` for the `github` backend.\\n3. `backends/github_contents._GitHubContentsStore` \u2014 content-addressed storage on the `dh-content` branch. Constructed by `GitHubBackend` and described there as authoritative. Observably live.\\n\\nThis supersedes the earlier statement in this issue that the Gist provider is the live GitHub path. Whether `create_artifact_provider` and the Gist provider are still reachable at all, or are a second dead design alongside the comment one, is not established here \u2014 that is now the first question to answer.\\n\\nSize behaviour follows from that: whatever limit applies is a property of the content-addressed store, not of the 64 KiB comment cap and not of the Gist path. No size behaviour for the live store has been established.\\n\\nThe pattern this issue is really about is now clearer. Three storage designs coexist in one module tree; the one with the most explicit documentation and the most test coverage is the one nothing calls, and the authoritative one is named as such only inside a module docstring. Anyone reasoning about artifact storage from the obvious entry point reaches the wrong design \u2014 which has already happened twice while filing this issue.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"745afa2e282aa87aac3050944ccff728e5782ceea10da4277f9678fe07e469a1\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABSPdiuw\"}"}